@page "/about"
@model AboutModel
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = SiteText.NavAbout;
    var site = SiteText.SiteName;
}
@functions {
    // The contact as text, or linked when Site:ContactUrl is set.
    private Microsoft.AspNetCore.Html.IHtmlContent ContactHtml() =>
        Model.ContactUrl is null
            ? new Microsoft.AspNetCore.Html.HtmlString(SiteHtml.Encode(Model.Contact))
            : new Microsoft.AspNetCore.Html.HtmlString($"<a href=\"{SiteHtml.Encode(Model.ContactUrl)}\">{SiteHtml.Encode(Model.Contact)}</a>");
}
<article class="prose">
    <h1>About @site</h1>

    <h2>What this site is</h2>
    <p>@site is an unofficial website for looking up IUCN Red List assessments and citing them on English Wikipedia. It is not affiliated with or endorsed by IUCN. It is free and has no ads.</p>
    <p>Each taxon on the IUCN Red List has a page with:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>the category, criteria and population trend of its latest global assessment, with the date assessed and the year published</li>
        <li>wikitext to copy into Wikipedia: a {{cite iucn}} citation, an {{IUCN status}} template, and the taxobox status parameters (status, status_system and status_ref), labelled with the taxobox the article most likely uses: {{Speciesbox}} for a species, {{Subspeciesbox}} for an animal subspecies, and {{Infraspeciesbox}} for a plant or fungus subspecies or a variety</li>
        <li>a {{cite Q}} citation of the assessment's Wikidata item, or QuickStatements commands to create that item</li>
        <li>its earlier global assessments, and its latest assessment in each region</li>
        <li>common names and synonyms</li>
        <li>links to the assessment on the IUCN Red List website, the English Wikipedia article, the Wikidata item and the Catalogue of Life page, and for Australian species the SPRAT profile and the listing under Australia's EPBC Act</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Some taxa with no current assessment also have pages: old IUCN ids of taxa that now have another id, and taxa that IUCN no longer assesses. These pages show the taxon's earlier assessments, with wikitext for each.</p>
    <p>Search matches scientific names, common names in any language, and synonyms.</p>

    <h2>Who runs this site</h2>
    <p>This site is run by an English Wikipedia editor who also runs the Wikipedia bot account Beastie Bot.</p>
    <p>To report a wrong citation, a wrong link or another problem with this site, contact @ContactHtml(). Include the address of the taxon page.</p>
    <p>If a category or another detail is also wrong on the IUCN Red List website, the error is in IUCN's data. Report it to IUCN through the IUCN Red List website.</p>

    <h2>Data sources</h2>
    <p>This site reformats and combines the data from these sources.</p>
    <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th scope="col">Source</th>
                    <th scope="col">Used for</th>
                    <th scope="col">Licence</th>
                    <th scope="col">Version</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <th scope="row"><a href="@SiteText.IucnRedListUrl">IUCN Red List</a></th>
                    <td>Scientific names, common names, synonyms, classification, and global and regional assessments: category, criteria, population trend, dates, authors and IUCN's citation text</td>
                    <td><a href="@SiteText.TermsUrl">IUCN Red List Terms of Use</a></td>
                    <td>Version @Model.Version.@if (Model.ApiDateRange is not null) {<text> Assessment details downloaded from the IUCN Red List API @Model.ApiDateRange.</text>}</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <th scope="row"><a href="@Model.GbifUrl">GBIF: IUCN checklist, published by IUCN</a></th>
                    <td>DOIs of the latest global assessments</td>
                    <td><a href="@SiteText.LicenceCcBy">CC BY 4.0</a></td>
                    <td>@Model.GbifVersion</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <th scope="row"><a href="@SiteText.WikidataUrl">Wikidata</a></th>
                    <td>Wikidata items of taxa and of assessments, common names, and DOIs not found in IUCN's citation text or GBIF</td>
                    <td><a href="@SiteText.LicenceCc0">CC0</a></td>
                    <td>@(Model.BuiltDate is null ? null : SiteText.CachedUpTo(Model.BuiltDate))</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <th scope="row"><a href="@SiteText.CrossrefUrl">Crossref</a></th>
                    <td>DOIs not found in IUCN's citation text, GBIF or Wikidata, taken from Crossref's list of the DOIs IUCN has registered. For recent assessments not in that list, this site checks possible DOIs at doi.org.</td>
                    <td><a href="@SiteText.CrossrefLicenceUrl">CC0</a></td>
                    <td>@(Model.DoiCheckedDate is null ? null : SiteText.DoisCheckedUpTo(Model.DoiCheckedDate))</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <th scope="row"><a href="@SiteText.EnglishWikipediaUrl">English Wikipedia</a></th>
                    <td>English common names (from article titles and taxoboxes) and links to Wikipedia articles</td>
                    <td><a href="@SiteText.LicenceCcBySa">CC BY-SA 4.0</a></td>
                    <td>@(Model.BuiltDate is null ? null : SiteText.CachedUpTo(Model.BuiltDate))</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <th scope="row"><a href="@Model.ColUrl">Catalogue of Life</a></th>
                    <td>English common names, synonyms, and links to Catalogue of Life pages</td>
                    <td><a href="@SiteText.LicenceCcBy">CC BY 4.0</a></td>
                    <td>@Model.ColRelease</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <th scope="row"><a href="@SiteText.SpratUrl">Species Profile and Threats Database (SPRAT)</a>, @SiteText.SpratLicensor</th>
                    <td>Links to SPRAT profiles, and EPBC Act listings of Australian species</td>
                    <td><a href="@SiteText.LicenceCcBy">CC BY 4.0</a></td>
                    <td>@Model.SpratDate</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <h3>Citations of the data sources</h3>
    <ul class="source-citations">
        <li><strong>GBIF:</strong> @Html.Raw(AboutModel.CitationHtml(Model.GbifCitation ?? "IUCN checklist on GBIF, published by IUCN.", Model.GbifUrl))</li>
        <li><strong>Catalogue of Life:</strong> @Html.Raw(AboutModel.CitationHtml(Model.ColCitation ?? (Model.ColRelease is null ? "Catalogue of Life." : $"Catalogue of Life, release {Model.ColRelease}."), Model.ColUrl))</li>
        <li><strong>SPRAT:</strong> @Html.Raw(AboutModel.CitationHtml($"Species Profile and Threats Database, {SiteText.SpratLicensor}.", SiteText.SpratUrl))</li>
    </ul>
    <p>For the citation of the IUCN Red List, see <a href="#iucn-terms">IUCN Red List Terms of Use</a>.</p>

    <h2>How the wikitext is made</h2>
    <p><strong>Authors.</strong> IUCN gives the authors of an assessment as a single list, such as "Wiig, Ø., Amstrup, S. &amp; Thiemann, G.". This site splits the list into one parameter for each author. About 1% of author names cannot be split with confidence. Those names are given exactly as IUCN wrote them, and a note under the citation asks you to check them.</p>
    <p><strong>DOI.</strong> The DOI comes from the first of these sources that has one:</p>
    <ol>
        <li>IUCN's own citation text, which includes a DOI for about 12% of latest assessments</li>
        <li>GBIF's copy of the IUCN checklist</li>
        <li>Wikidata</li>
        <li>Crossref's list of the DOIs IUCN has registered, and for recent assessments missing from it, possible DOIs checked at doi.org</li>
    </ol>
    <p>A note under the citation says when the DOI came from GBIF, Wikidata, Crossref or doi.org, or when no DOI was found. {{cite iucn}} works without a DOI.</p>
    <p><strong>Access date.</strong> By default, <code>|access-date=</code> is the date this site downloaded the details of the assessment from the IUCN Red List API@(Model.ApiDateRange is null ? "" : ", " + Model.ApiDateRange). If you check the assessment on the IUCN Red List website yourself, choose today's date. This site gives today's date in UTC. You can also leave the access date out.</p>
    <p><strong>Citation options.</strong> On each taxon page you can choose:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>the author format: <code>|author=Surname, I.</code> with <code>|author2=</code>, <code>|author3=</code> and so on, which most articles use, or <code>|last1=</code> and <code>|first1=</code>, which the {{cite iucn}} documentation shows</li>
        <li>whether to use the authors' full given names instead of initials. This option is shown when IUCN gives the full given names of at least one author of the assessment. By default, the names are written with initials, as in IUCN's citation.</li>
        <li>the access date: the date downloaded from IUCN (the default), today's date in UTC, or no access date</li>
        <li>whether to wrap the citation in <code>&lt;ref&gt;</code> tags, and the ref name. The default ref name is "iucn" for the latest global assessment, "iucn" and the year published for an earlier global assessment (such as "iucn2008"), and "iucn-" and the region for a regional assessment (such as "iucn-europe"), so citations of different assessments in one article have different names.</li>
        <li>whether to add <code>|name-list-style=amp</code>, which puts "&amp;" before the last author, as IUCN does</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The wikitext changes as soon as you change an option. In a browser without JavaScript, select <strong>@SiteText.UpdateWikitext</strong> to load the page with the new wikitext.</p>
    <p><strong>Citing through Wikidata.</strong> {{cite Q}} cites a work from the details stored in its Wikidata item. When this site's data has a Wikidata item for an assessment, the taxon page gives {{cite Q}} wikitext for that item, with the same ref options as {{cite iucn}}. It has an access date only when the item has a URL for the assessment, because {{cite Q}} reports an error for an access date without a URL. If the item is missing statements that this site can supply, such as the DOI or the authors, the page also gives QuickStatements commands that add them. When this site's data has no item for the assessment, the page gives QuickStatements commands that create one like the assessment items already on Wikidata: a scholarly article published in the IUCN Red List, with the taxon as its main subject.</p>
    <p>QuickStatements makes the edits with your own Wikidata account. This site never edits Wikidata.</p>
    <p>English Wikipedia's guidance on {{cite Q}}: check every detail that it takes from Wikidata, and don't use it in an article whose citations mostly give authors as “Last, First” or in Vancouver style. See <a href="@SiteText.CiteQGuidanceUrl">Wikipedia:Citing sources</a>.</p>
    <p><strong>Earlier assessments.</strong> To cite an earlier assessment, select <strong>@SiteText.ShowWikitext</strong> in its row of the Assessment history table. The page then shows the wikitext for that assessment and says which assessment it is.</p>

    <h2 id="iucn-terms">IUCN Red List Terms of Use</h2>
    <p>The IUCN Red List data on this site is used under the <a href="@SiteText.TermsUrl">IUCN Red List Terms of Use</a> and remains subject to those terms.</p>
    <p>Cite the IUCN Red List as: IUCN @(Model.ReleaseYear ?? "?"). The IUCN Red List of Threatened Species. Version @Model.Version. https://www.iucnredlist.org</p>
    <p>The terms do not allow IUCN data to be redistributed, so this site has no downloads.</p>

    <h2>Full assessments and data downloads</h2>
    <p>Each taxon page on this site shows a summary of the assessment. The full assessment, including the rationale, threats and conservation actions, is on the IUCN Red List website at <a href="https://www.iucnredlist.org">https://www.iucnredlist.org</a>. Each taxon page links to its assessment there.</p>
    <p>To download IUCN Red List data, use the IUCN Red List website. Downloads there need an account and are subject to IUCN's terms.</p>
</article>
